## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by **reference**.

Every render creates new references for inline objects/functions, so even if the data looks identical, the child sees a "changed" prop and re-renders.

```jsx
// Bad — new object reference on every render
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// Bad — new function reference on every render
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.
Use it on **pure components** that receive stable props and render often.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ label, onClick }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>);
```

> Without stable prop references, `React.memo` buys you nothing.

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a **function** across renders.
Use it when passing callbacks as props to memoized children.

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a **computed value** across renders.
Use it for expensive calculations or to stabilize object references passed as props.

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme, fontSize }), [theme, fontSize]);
```

**When NOT to use them:** Don't sprinkle these everywhere — they have overhead. Only apply them when profiling shows a real problem, or when the memoization boundary is clearly load-bearing.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every time a context `value` changes reference, **all consumers re-render** — regardless of which part of the value they actually use.

```jsx
// Bad — new object on every render of AuthProvider
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fixes:**
- Memoize the value with `useMemo`.
- Split contexts: separate `UserContext` (changes often) from `UserDispatchContext` (stable), so read-only consumers don't re-render on mutations.

```jsx
const UserContext = createContext(null);
const UserDispatchContext = createContext(null);

function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserDispatchContext.Provider value={setUser}>  {/* stable */}
      <UserContext.Provider value={user}>           {/* changes */}
        {children}
      </UserContext.Provider>
    </UserDispatchContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `List` re-renders on every parent render even when items haven't changed:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <List
        items={['a', 'b', 'c']}          // new array every render
        onSelect={(item) => log(item)}    // new function every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

function List({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map(i => <div onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
}
```

**After** — stable references let `React.memo` do its job:

```jsx
const ITEMS = ['a', 'b', 'c'];  // stable, defined outside component

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => log(item), []);  // stable

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <List items={ITEMS} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(function List({ items, onSelect }) {
  return items.map(i => <div onClick={() => onSelect(i)}>{i}</div>);
});
```

Now clicking "Increment" re-renders `Parent` but **skips `List` entirely**.

---

**Mental model:** memoization only works when the inputs are stable. Fix references first (`useCallback`, `useMemo`, module-level constants), then add `React.memo` at the boundary.
